{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"advisory":[{"agreement":1,"block_threshold":0.54,"blocking_enabled":true,"checklist_item":"- [ ] Reconcile _classify_write_error's fallback with decode_acli_triple's assignee_error class so a deterministic assignee/permission failure is not mapped to retryable_deferred.","criteria":["correctness","maintainability"],"decision":"advisory","evidence":["summary_operation.py:110-116 `_classify_write_error` handles only AcliAuthError/AcliMutationError/AcliTimeoutError, else `return Disposition.retryable_deferred`","acli_subprocess.py:450-451 decode_acli_triple returns `AcliOutcome.assignee_error` for assignee stderr","acli_subprocess.py:600-603 `_run_acli` re-raises a bare CalledProcessError (not a typed error) for assignee-permission/not-found stderr"],"finding":"decode_acli_triple can classify a nonzero exit as AcliOutcome.assignee_error, but _classify_write_error (the write-fault mapper in summary_operation) has no assignee branch and maps such faults to retryable_deferred. The two classifiers disagree on the same underlying ACLI failure surface; an assignee-class failure would be treated as transient/retryable by the write path rather than permanent.","id":"0","impact":0.27,"location":"src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/adapters/jira/summary_operation.py:99","norm_id":"n217a13b87d510daa","priority":0.27,"reason":"default-advisory","scenarios":["A summary edit that surfaces an assignee-permission stderr on nonzero exit would be classified retryable_deferred by _classify_write_error and re-driven by the budget rather than treated as a permanent rejection."],"severity":"minor","suggested_fix":"","tier":"LLM","validity":1.0,"verification":{"binary":{"absence_confirmed_in_context":"yes","asserted_cap